T2.5 / T2.6 bulk reductions vs OpenCV -- countNonZero / countAnd / countAndSplit
================================================================================

DENOMINATOR (ARCHITECTURE 10.3): cv::countNonZero on the SAME binary content
stored as CV_8U -- what a user runs today without binCV. For countAnd, the
composite that call site would have to write instead: cv::bitwise_and into a
temporary, then cv::countNonZero. A third row measures BinMat::countNonZero(),
the per-pixel loop this task was supposed to improve on.

PLATFORM: x86_64, native, taskset -c 2, Release (-O2 -DNDEBUG).
  INDICATIVE ONLY (EXPERIMENTS.md "Measurement platforms"). x86_64 is the wrong
  ISA for every question this table touches: D-6 rests on aarch64 instruction
  latency, and E-1/E-2/E-3 are cache questions that close on the reference
  device through scripts/run_on_pi.sh. NOTHING HERE CLOSES AN EXPERIMENT.

TWO RUNS, because the popcount lowering changes the answer by 5-7x and binCV's
own default build gets the slow one -- the finding recorded as EXPERIMENTS.md
X-7:

  run A: the shipping portable baseline (no -march / -mpopcnt), which is what
         bincv-cpp/CMakeLists.txt builds. GCC lowers __builtin_popcountll to a
         CALL to libgcc's __popcountdi2, once per word. (Clang inlines a ~15
         instruction SWAR sequence instead; neither is popcntq.)
  run B: the same source with -mpopcnt, i.e. what it compiles to when the build
         is allowed to assume SSE4.2. `popcntq` per word.

RUN B IS NOT A PROPOSED DEFAULT. Enabling an ISA baseline is a dispatch decision
(ROADMAP 2.3, Phase 5) and no experiment has settled it. It is here so the run A
numbers can be attributed to the lowering rather than to the algorithm.

HEADLINE at 640x480, from the two runs below:

                              run A (as shipped)   run B (-mpopcnt)
  binCV uint64 countNonZero    0.04544 ns/px        0.00639 ns/px
  cv::countNonZero  CV_8U      0.01098 ns/px        0.01304 ns/px
  ratio                        0.24x -- binCV is    2.04x -- binCV is
                               4.2x SLOWER          2.0x faster
  vs BinMat::countNonZero()    6.1x faster          35.3x faster
  buffer                       38400 B vs 307200 B (8x, either way)

So the algorithm is 7x off the same algorithm's own potential, and the whole
difference is the lowering of one builtin. The bulk API (D-6) is what makes that
fixable in ops/reduce.hpp alone; a per-word popcount in the public surface would
have put the same call in every caller's loop instead.

